DP  - 1999 Feb 1
TI  - Exfoliative dermatitis.
PG  - 625-30
AB  - Exfoliative dermatitis, also known as erythroderma, is an uncommon but serious 
      skin disorder that family physicians must be able to recognize and treat 
      appropriately. Although the etiology is often unknown, exfoliative dermatitis may 
      be the result of a drug reaction or an underlying malignancy. The approach to 
      treatment should include discontinuation of any potentially causative medications 
      and a search for any underlying malignancy. One of the most common malignancies 
      associated with exfoliative dermatitis is cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, which may 
      not manifest for months or even years after the onset of the skin condition. 
      Hospitalization is usually necessary for initial evaluation and treatment. In the 
      hospital, special attention must be given to maintaining temperature control, 
      replacing lost fluids and electrolytes, and preventing and treating infection. 
      The long-term prognosis is good in patients with drug-induced disease, although 
      the course tends to be remitting and relapsing in idiopathic cases. The prognosis 
      of cases associated with malignancy typically depends on the outcome of the 
      underlying malignancy.
