VI  - 13
IP  - 9
DP  - 1999 Nov
TI  - Angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor-induced angioedema: a report of two 
      cases.
PG  - 917-9
AB  - Angioedema is a rare but potentially fatal side effect of angiotensin converting 
      enzyme (ACE) inhibitors. We report for the first time, two children with systemic 
      lupus erythematosus who developed acute angioedema after the long-term use of 
      enalapril. Prompt recognition and appropriate management of ACE-induced 
      angioedema prevented life-threatening complications. This report highlights the 
      potential risks of angioedema associated with the use of ACE inhibitors in 
      children. Patients should be advised to seek medical treatment immediately if 
      they experience swelling of the face, neck, or tongue, and especially if they 
      have trouble breathing, speaking, or swallowing.
