DP  - 1999 Nov
TI  - Profound bradycardia and hypotension following spinal anaesthesia in a patient 
      receiving an ACE inhibitor: an important 'drug' interaction?
PG  - 796-8
AB  - An 86-year-old man on whom a transurethral resection of prostate was performed 
      under spinal anaesthesia developed profound bradycardia and hypotension with 
      disturbance of consciousness during transfer to the recovery room. Initial 
      treatment with atropine produced rapid improvement in cardiovascular and cerebral 
      function. A further hypotensive episode (without bradycardia) occurred 
      approximately 1 h later but responded rapidly to methoxamine. The patient made a 
      full recovery during an overnight stay on the High Dependency Unit. Possible 
      mechanisms for this event are discussed, with the proposal that the concomitant 
      administration of captopril and the relative unavailability of Angiotensin II may 
      have significantly contributed to the problem.
