VI  - 2
IP  - 3
DP  - 2000 Sep
TI  - First-dose hypotension after angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors in 
      chronic heart failure: a comparison of enalapril and perindopril. Slovak 
      Investigator Group.
PG  - 299-304
AB  - BACKGROUND: First-dose hypotension refers to an observed reduction in blood 
      pressure after the administration of the first dose of ACE inhibitors in patients 
      with congestive heart failure. AIM: To compare the first-dose responses of 
      low-dose enalapril and perindopril in patients with stable symptomatic chronic 
      heart failure. METHODS: Single blind, randomised, multicenter, parallel, 
      prospective study. Patients (N=298) with chronic heart failure due to ischemic 
      heart disease or dilated cardiomyopathy, NYHA II-IV, ejection fraction<40%, 
      age>18 years, naive to ACE inhibitors or ATI-receptor blocker, were randomised to 
      receive a single dose of 2. 5 mg enalapril or 2.0 mg perindopril. Baseline 
      laboratory and clinical examinations were performed before entry into the study. 
      Ambulatory blood pressure monitoring started 2 h before the study medication was 
      given, and continued for at least 10 h after the medication. RESULTS: The maximum 
      drop in blood pressure appeared approximately 4 h after dose administration in 
      both groups, and was more pronounced in the enalapril group. Patients in the 
      enalapril group had a significantly higher incidence of asymptomatic hypotension. 
      No symptomatic hypotension requiring a change in medication or a prolongation of 
      hospitalisation was observed. CONCLUSION: A low dose of perindopril is 
      well-tolerated at initiation of ACE inhibitor therapy in patients with chronic 
      heart failure and causes less first-dose hypotension than a low dose of 
      enalapril.
