TI  - Emergency room management of hypertensive urgencies and emergencies.
PG  - 158-64
AB  - Hypertensive crisis affects upward of 500,000 Americans each year. Although the 
      incidence of hypertensive crisis is low, affecting fewer than 1% of hypertensive 
      adults, more than 50 million adult Americans suffer from hypertension. 
      Presentation of a patient with severe hypertension to the emergency room demands 
      immediate evaluation, prompt recognition of a hypertensive emergency or urgency, 
      and the prompt institution of appropriate therapeutic measures to prevent 
      progression of target-organ damage and to avoid a catastrophic event. 
      Hypertensive emergencies are severe elevations in blood pressure that are 
      complicated by evidence of progressive target-organ dysfunction such as coronary 
      ischemia, disordered cerebral function, a cerebrovascular event, pulmonary edema, 
      or renal failure. Although therapy with parenteral antihypertensive agents may be 
      initiated in the emergency department, these patients warrant prompt admission to 
      an intensive care unit where continuous monitoring of blood pressure can be 
      assured during therapy.
