DP  - 2001 Jul
TI  - The kidney in congestive heart failure: renal adverse event rate of treatment.
PG  - 99-107
AB  - In this paper we review the effect of medical treatment on renal function in 
      patients with congestive heart failure. We have examined data from the 
      large-scale heart failure studies with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, 
      angiotensin receptor blockers, calcium channel blockers, beta-receptor blockers, 
      an aldosterone antagonist, and a vasopeptidase inhibitor. Renal outcome was 
      reported in almost all of the studies with angiotensin-converting enzyme 
      inhibitors. Despite concern about renal adverse events with drugs in this class, 
      they seem to be safe in patients with congestive heart failure. In contrast, we 
      did not find any report about renal function in patients treated with 
      beta-receptor blockers for congestive heart failure.
