DP  - 2001 Sep
TI  - What is the relevance of the HOPE study in general practice?
PG  - 449-57
AB  - The unique findings from the HOPE (Heart Outcomes Prevention Evaluation) study 
      strongly support extending the use of the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) 
      inhibitor ramipril as a preventive agent for patients at high risk of 
      cardiovascular events with normal left ventricular function. In addition, 
      ramipril provides significant benefit in diabetic patients. These findings will 
      impact on how ramipril is used in primary care, where ACE inhibitors are 
      currently underprescribed. Patients reflecting the inclusion criteria of the HOPE 
      study should be considered as suitable candidates for long-term ramipril therapy 
      as an addition to their existing drug regimen. Screening should include control 
      of kidney function (by serum creatinine), particularly within the first two weeks 
      of treatment, in addition to regular monitoring of serum potassium. However, the 
      HOPE study shows that ramipril is well tolerated at high doses and over a long 
      treatment period. The effectiveness of therapy should also be regularly reviewed 
      and dose adjustments made where necessary. If concern remains, referral to a 
      specialist--a cardiologist or a diabetologist--may ultimately be necessary.
