VI  - 163
IP  - 50
DP  - 2001 Dec 10
TI  - [Angiotensin II antagonists versus ACE inhibitors in the treatment of raised 
      blood pressure].
PG  - 7036-9
AB  - Blockade of the renin-angiotensin system has proved an important principle in the 
      pharmacotherapy of cardiovascular diseases. There is now overwhelming 
      documentation on the beneficial effect of angiotensin converting enzyme 
      inhibitors (ACE inhibitors) on mortality and morbidity in patients with 
      cardiovascular risk factors. Such documentation is still lacking for the 
      angiotensin II receptor antagonists which, in 1999, sold for 77% of the amount 
      spent on ACE inhibitors in Denmark. This review assesses the therapeutic effects 
      of angiotensin II antagonists compared with the ACE inhibitors in patients with 
      uncomplicated essential hypertension. From 21 comparative studies with a duration 
      of up to one year, it can be concluded that the lowering of the blood pressure 
      with the two classes is similar. Both showed relatively few side effects, the 
      only difference being a higher incidence of cough after ACE inhibitors. Until 
      larger comparative long-term studies on mortality and morbidity have been 
      conducted, angiotensin II antagonists should only be used in hypertensive 
      patients experiencing unacceptable side effects with ACE inhibitors.
