VI  - 5
IP  - 5
DP  - 2003 Oct
TI  - Use of calcium antagonists in renal patients: therapeutic benefit or medical 
      malpractice?
PG  - 430-6
AB  - In patients with kidney disease, control of hypertension is paramount in helping 
      to slow down the progression of both diabetic and nondiabetic nephropathy. A 
      significant amount of data have been published that suggest blockade of the 
      renin-angiotensin system should be considered as first-line therapy for patients 
      with kidney disease. Meanwhile, some studies have suggested that the use of 
      calcium channel blockers may have deleterious effects on patients with kidney 
      disease. This manuscript reviews the renal outcomes of trials in which calcium 
      channel blockers were included in the management of patients with and without 
      kidney disease. The data suggest that agents that block the renin-angiotensin 
      system are superior to calcium channel blockers in protecting against progressive 
      kidney disease. However, there is no conclusive evidence that calcium channel 
      blockers are injurious to the kidney, and they may be particularly beneficial in 
      post-renal transplant patients.
