TI  - Pharmacologic drugs for controlled hypotension.
PG  - 326-37
AB  - Due to the risks of transfusion reactions and the transmission of infectious 
      diseases, there has been increased interest in measures to limit intraoperative 
      blood loss and avoid the need for homologous transfusion. Controlled hypotension 
      is one technique that has been used to limit intraoperative blood loss. Several 
      drugs have been used alone or in combination for controlled hypotension, 
      including the inhalational anesthetics, direct acting vasodilators such as 
      nitroglycerin and nitroprusside, beta adrenergic antagonists, and calcium channel 
      blockers. Various drugs available to the clinician for controlled hypotension are 
      reviewed.
