VI  - 32
IP  - 12
DP  - 1994 Dec
TI  - Profound hypotension in a tetraplegic patient following angiotensin-converting 
      enzyme inhibitor lisinopril. Case report.
PG  - 871-4
AB  - We present the case of a 60 year old C6 complete tetraplegic patient who 
      developed profound hypotension following initiation of the angiotensin-converting 
      enzyme inhibitor lisinopril to control blood pressure. Other causes of 
      hypotension, such as myocardial infarction and sepsis was ruled out. Inhibition 
      of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system was the probable cause of 
      hypotension. This case demonstrates the critical importance of the 
      renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis in the maintenance of blood pressure in 
      tetraplegic patients, who may lack input from the brain to sympathetic neurons, 
      and therefore have increased reliance on the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis 
      for the maintenance of blood pressure. Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors 
      should be avoided in tetraplegic patients, unless other treatment modalities are 
      ineffective.
