TI  - Oral captopril versus placebo among 13,634 patients with suspected acute 
      myocardial infarction: interim report from the Chinese Cardiac Study (CCS-1).
PG  - 686-7
AB  - 13,634 patients entering 650 Chinese hospitals up to 36 h after the onset of 
      suspected acute myocardial infarction (MI) were randomised between one month of 
      oral captopril (6.25 mg initial dose, 12.5 mg 2 h later, and then 12.5 mg three 
      times daily) or matching placebo. Captopril was associated with a non-significant 
      reduction in 4-week mortality (617 [9.05%] captopril-allocated vs 654 [9.59%] 
      placebo-allocated deaths; 2p = 0.3). There was a significant excess of 
      hypotension, mostly early after the start of treatment, but no evidence of any 
      adverse effect on early mortality (even among patients who were hypotensive at 
      entry). Taken together with the other trials of converting enzyme inhibitors 
      started early in acute MI, these results indicate that such therapy is generally 
      safe and typically prevents about 5 deaths per 1000 patients treated for the 
      first month.
