DP  - 1993 Nov-Dec
TI  - Sodium cromoglycate: a remedy for ACE inhibitor-induced cough.
PG  - 319-20
AB  - The beneficial effects of angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors may be 
      limited by cough, for which the only consistently effective treatment is 
      withdrawal of therapy. The effect of inhaled sodium cromoglycate (20 mg, 4 times 
      daily) was examined in five patients who experienced cough following the 
      introduction of an ACE inhibitor. In three patients inhaled cromoglycate 
      suppressed the cough. Inhaled sodium cromoglycate may thus be of value in a 
      proportion of patients with ACE inhibitor-induced cough.
