VI  - 87
IP  - 3
DP  - 1994 Mar
TI  - Coroner's cases of death due to errors in prescribing or giving medicines or to 
      adverse drug reactions: Birmingham 1986-1991.
PG  - 145-8
AB  - The records for Coroner's Inquests in one district during a 6 year period were 
      examined retrospectively to establish the number and nature of deaths which were 
      due to errors in the prescription or administration of medicines, and those due 
      to adverse drug reactions. The district has a population of 1.19 million (1991), 
      and a total of 3277 inquests were opened during the period 1986-1991. Ten of the 
      deaths were identified as due to errors of prescribing or giving drugs. During 
      the same period, 36 deaths were caused by adverse drug reactions. These 46 deaths 
      made up approximately one in 2000 of all deaths during the study period. About a 
      fifth of deaths related to prescribing and administering drugs are due to errors 
      and may be more easily preventable than deaths due to adverse reactions.
