DP  - 1995 Nov
TI  - Angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor therapy and acute pancreatitis.
PG  - 369-71
AB  - Angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors are generally well tolerated. 
      Worldwide, only few reports have been published associating pancreatitis with ACE 
      inhibitor therapy. We report a case in whom there was no other likely explanation 
      for the acute pancreatitis than enalapril therapy, which was temporally 
      associated with the symptoms. Possible mechanisms underlying the induction of 
      pancreatitis by ACE inhibitors are discussed. With the increasing use of ACE 
      inhibitors, the incidence of rare adverse effects such as potentially lethal 
      pancreatitis is likely to increase. Clinicians need to be aware of this 
      association.
