IS  - 0114-5916 (Linking)
VI  - 14
IP  - 6
DP  - 1996 Jun
TI  - Adverse metabolic effects of antihypertensive drugs. Implications for treatment.
PG  - 355-64
AB  - Adverse metabolic effects have been associated with drugs used in the therapy of 
      hypertension, especially diuretics and beta-blockers. These effects include 
      electrolyte, glucose/insulin, lipid and uric acid disturbances. This may explain, 
      at least in part, why early trials examining the impact of antihypertensive 
      pharmacotherapy with diuretics and beta-blockers showed beneficial effects on 
      coronary artery disease that fell disappointingly short of the predicted effect. 
      Among therapeutic drugs, diuretics cause disturbances in electrolyte homeostasis, 
      e.g. hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, and hyponatraemia. In contrast, ACE 
      inhibitors cause hyperkalaemia under certain circumstances. Both diuretics and 
      beta-blockers, especially nonselective beta-blockers that lack intrinsic 
      sympathomimetic capabilities, have been associated with disturbances in 
      glucose/insulin metabolism and can cause deleterious alterations in the profile 
      of circulating plasma lipids. Hyperuricaemia, associated with diuretic use, 
      appears to be a problem only in those patients who are predisposed to high 
      circulating levels of uric acid.
