DP  - 1995 Dec
TI  - A comparison of the effect of enalapril and metoprolol on renal function, 
      potassium balance, lipid profile, cardiac function, exercise tolerance and 
      quality of life in hypertensive dialysis patients.
PG  - 757-62
AB  - 26 patients with hypertension while on hemodialysis or continuous ambulatory 
      peritoneal dialysis for end-stage renal diseases were treated first with 
      enalapril and then changed to metoprolol. Both drugs were shown to be similarly 
      effective in controlling blood pressure. There was no difference between the two 
      drugs in their effects on renal function, potassium balance, lipid profile, 
      cardiac function, exercise tolerance, and quality of life. Mild worsening of 
      anemia was observed during treatment with enalapril. No serious side effects were 
      reported. Use of enalapril is safe in the treatment of hypertension in dialysis 
      patients.
