TI  - A multicenter, parallel comparative study of the antihypertensive efficacy of 
      once-daily lisinopril vs enalapril with 24-h ambulatory blood pressure monitoring 
      in essential hypertension.
PG  - 837-41
AB  - The aim of the study was to compare the antihypertensive efficacy of once-daily 
      lisinopril vs enalapril both during normal daily activity and sleep, in 
      mild-to-moderate essential hypertension. After a 4-week wash-out period, 34 
      patients (17 M, 17 F) aged 22 to 67 years were randomized in a multicenter, open, 
      parallel fashion: 17 received lisinopril (10-20 mg) and 17 enalapril (10-20 mg) 
      for a 12-week period. Twenty-four hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring 
      (ABPM) was performed using an oscillometric non-invasive automated device at both 
      the end of the 4-week drug-free baseline period and during the last week of 
      treatment. With no differences in initial blood pressure (BP) between groups, 
      both drugs significantly reduced office and ABPM values. Lisinopril tended to 
      reduce BP in a greater extension than enalapril, but only the reduction of office 
      systolic BP (SBP) (p = 0.0062), 24-h SBP load (P = 0.0182) and night time SBP 
      load (P = 0.0316) reached statistical significance. We conclude that, in spite of 
      a more prominent reduction of SBP by lisinopril, both drugs have a similar 
      efficacy in reducing BP, assessed by both office and ABPM measurements.
