TI  - Congestive heart failure in elderly patients. The treatment goal is improved 
      quality, not quantity, of life.
PG  - 207-8, 211-5
AB  - Primary care physicians who see elderly patients are likely to see cases of 
      congestive heart failure, since this condition is typically the result of 
      long-standing hypertension or coronary artery disease. Recognizing the condition 
      in elderly patients may not be easy, though, because clinical signs can be 
      distorted by accompanying symptoms. In this article, the authors discuss 
      pathophysiologic, diagnostic, and pharmacokinetic issues. They also describe 
      therapy with diuretics, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, and digoxin and 
      outline special considerations in the elderly.
