DP  - 1998 Jul
TI  - Perindopril postmarketing surveillance: a 12 month study in 47,351 hypertensive 
      patients.
PG  - 63-70
AB  - AIMS: To gain information on serious adverse events in a large number of patients 
      exposed to perindopril. METHODS: Four thousand seven hundred and eighty-eight 
      general practitioners throughout France collaborated in the recruitment of 47,351 
      patients for a 12 month postmarketing study. Data collection was undertaken by 
      company representatives under the supervision of nine regional medical officers. 
      Computerised data entry was performed by six pharmaceutical officers. Serious 
      adverse events were later individually reviewed medically. RESULTS: Withdrawals 
      due to adverse events occurred in 6.1% of female and 3.2% of males patients. The 
      ascertainment of adverse events in this study approved satisfactory, as shown by 
      the reported incidence of cough, which was 11.3% in women and 7.8% in men, this 
      being compatible with the best estimates of the true incidence of cough during 
      ACE-inhibitor therapy. Serious adverse reactions-anaphylaxis and blood 
      dyscrasias-were rare. CONCLUSIONS: This study successfully followed a large 
      cohort of patients treated with perindopril and failed to demonstrate any 
      unexpected hazards.
